// What a Khayyam-written compiler needs first: the text of the unit it is
// compiling. The language has no primitive type, so a text is a capsule whose
// methods come from outside, and a compiler that cannot see its own input's
// characters cannot begin. This probe asks the only question that matters here —
// can a program's own code reach a character of a text it holds — and the
// answer the target gives is recorded in bootstrap.test.ts.
//
// The language's position: no primitive types, and every value is wrapped in a
// named capsule (docs/khayyam/khayyam.md → Type Principles Realized).

tp Character cp {
}

tp Text cp {
}

tp CharacterAt mt (self Text) (at Count) (character Character)

tp Count cp {
}

vr zero Count
